\b\fs28 GA Q1 — Indian Tribal Communities\b0\fs22\par
Source: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Correct answer: C. Nagas.\b0\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sa80 \bullet\tab Nagas are associated mainly with Nagaland and north-eastern India.\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sa80 \bullet\tab Gonds are concentrated across central India, including Madhya Pradesh and Chhattisgarh.\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sa80 \bullet\tab Bhils are found mainly across Rajasthan, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh and Maharashtra.\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sa80 \bullet\tab Todas are an indigenous community of the Nilgiri Hills in Tamil Nadu.\par
\b Quick revision:\b0 Nagas—Nagaland; Todas—Nilgiris; Bhils and Gonds—western/central India.\par

\b\fs28 GA Q2 — Purvanchal Hills\b0\fs22\par
Source: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Correct answer: D. All three statements are correct.\b0\par
Purvanchal refers to eastern hill ranges associated with the Himalayan system in north-eastern India. It includes Patkai, Naga, Manipur and Mizo (Lushai) Hills. Therefore Patkai, Naga and Mizo Hills are all included.\par
\b Quick revision:\b0 Purvanchal = Patkai + Naga + Manipur + Mizo Hills.\par

\b\fs28 GA Q3 — Mughal Farman and East India Company\b0\fs22\par
Source label in repository: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Status: DISPUTED — excluded from score.\b0\par
The repository asks which Mughal emperor issued a farman granting the East India Company the right to trade in Bengal. Options: Akbar, Jahangir, Shah Jahan and Aurangzeb. Its explanation says the well-known 1717 farman was issued by Farrukhsiyar, who is missing from the options. It then claims Aurangzeb based on an earlier grant without identifying that grant or citing a source. The key and explanation conflict. Do not memorize Aurangzeb as verified until the original paper and answer key are checked.\par

\b\fs28 GA Q4 — Establishment of IMD\b0\fs22\par
Source: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Correct answer: A. 1875.\b0\par
The India Meteorological Department was established on 15 January 1875. Its work includes weather observations, forecasting, warnings and climate services.\par
\b Quick revision:\b0 IMD establishment date — 15 January 1875.\par

\b\fs28 GA Q5 — SI Unit of Pressure\b0\fs22\par
Source: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Correct answer: B. Pascal (Pa).\b0\par
Pressure is force per unit area: P = F/A. The SI unit is pascal, equal to one newton per square metre (N/m2). Newton is force, joule is energy/work and watt is power.\par
\b Formula:\b0 1 Pa = 1 N/m2.\par

\b\fs28 GA Q6 — Greenhouse Gases\b0\fs22\par
Source: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Correct answer: C. Nitrogen (N2).\b0\par
Major greenhouse gases include water vapour, carbon dioxide (CO2), methane (CH4), nitrous oxide (N2O) and ozone. Nitrogen (N2), which makes up most of dry air, is not considered a greenhouse gas because it does not absorb terrestrial infrared radiation effectively. Do not confuse nitrogen (N2) with nitrous oxide (N2O).\par
\b Quick revision:\b0 N2 is not a greenhouse gas; N2O is.\par

\b\fs28 GA Q7 — Buffer Solution\b0\fs22\par
Source: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Correct answer: B. No significant change in pH.\b0\par
A buffer solution resists a large change in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added. An acidic buffer commonly contains a weak acid and its conjugate base; a basic buffer commonly contains a weak base and its conjugate acid. Buffer capacity is limited; a sufficiently large addition can still change pH.\par
\b Quick revision:\b0 Buffer resists pH change; it does not keep pH absolutely constant.\par

\b\fs28 GA Q8 — First Person to Walk on the Moon\b0\fs22\par
Source: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Correct answer: C. Neil Armstrong.\b0\par
Neil Armstrong was the first person to walk on the Moon during Apollo 11 in July 1969. Buzz Aldrin was second. Michael Collins remained in lunar orbit in the command module. Yuri Gagarin was the first human in space, not the first person on the Moon.\par
\b Quick revision:\b0 Gagarin—first human in space; Armstrong—first to walk on the Moon.\par

\b\fs28 GA Q9 — Fundamental Rights\b0\fs22\par
Source: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Correct answer: B. Part III, Articles 12–35.\b0\par
Fundamental Rights are contained in Part III, Articles 12 to 35. Part IV (Articles 36–51) contains Directive Principles of State Policy. Part IV-A, Article 51A, contains Fundamental Duties. Part II (Articles 5–11) deals with citizenship.\par
\b Quick revision:\b0 Part III—Fundamental Rights; Part IV—DPSP; Part IV-A—Fundamental Duties.\par

\b\fs28 GA Q10 — Atmospheric Pressure Instrument\b0\fs22\par
Source: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Correct answer: B. Barometer.\b0\par
A barometer measures atmospheric pressure. A thermometer measures temperature; a hygrometer measures humidity; an anemometer measures wind speed.\par
\b Quick revision:\b0 Barometer—pressure; thermometer—temperature; hygrometer—humidity; anemometer—wind speed.\par

\b\fs28 GA Q11 — Vitamin D and Sunlight\b0\fs22\par
Source: SSC Scientific Assistant IMD GA 2017\par
\b Correct answer: D. Vitamin D.\b0\par
UVB radiation from sunlight helps the skin produce vitamin D3 from a cholesterol-derived precursor. Vitamin D supports calcium and phosphate regulation and bone health. Skin pigmentation, age, clothing, season and location affect production.\par
\b Quick revision:\b0 Sunlight is associated with vitamin D, not vitamins A, B12 or C.\par

